/* book: lead: A sensor does not jump to a new reading the instant the input changes: heat must flow, a mass must move, a diaphragm must flex. The delay is captured by a mathematical model: zero order (instant), first order (one energy store, one time constant τ) or second order (two energy stores, natural frequency ωn and damping ζ). These constants decide whether a thermocouple can follow a furnace or a cathet */
/* book Figure 1.5.1: Top: the τ = 2 s bead dipped from 25 °C into 100 °C reads 72.4 °C at 2 s and 96.3 °C at 6 s. Bottom left: the 1 Hz, ζ = 0.2 diaphragm overshoots to 1.527 at 0.51 s. Bottom right: the 25 Hz catheter doubles a 20 Hz harmonic at ζ = 0.2 (2.08) but passes it at 0.975 with ζ = 0.6. */
