/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Write the input as x(t) and output as y(t).
   - A zero-order sensor has H(s) = K, so y(t) = K x(t) at every instant.
   - A first-order sensor obeys τ(dy/dt) + y = Kx. τ is the time constant in seconds, dy/dt is the rate of change of output, and K is the static sensitivity.
   - For a step of size A at t = 0, starting from zero, y(t) = K A (1 − e−t/τ).
   - For a ramp input of slope R, the output becomes a ramp of the same slope after a start-up, but lags it by τR.
   - For a sinusoidal input x = A sin(ωt), the amplitude ratio is 1/√(1 + (ωτ)²) and the phase lag is tan⁻¹(ωτ).
   - A second-order sensor obeys (1/ωn²)(d²y/dt²) + (2ζ/ωn)(dy/dt) + y = Kx. ωn is the undamped natural frequency in rad/s; ζ is the damping ratio, dimensionless.
   - For a step of size A: when ζ < 1 the sensor is underdamped, the output rises past the final value and rings at the damped natural frequency ωd = ωn√(1 − ζ²) in rad/s.
   - For a sinusoid, put r = ω/ωn.
   - The four standard test inputs, side by side (unit sensitivity, starting from rest):
*/
