/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A measurement error is the difference between the reading and the true value: absolute error = xm − xtrue, where xm is the measured value and xtrue is the accepted true value.
   - The two error types respond differently to averaging.
   - Take n repeated readings x1, x2, …, xn at the same input.
   - s² is the sample variance, in squared units of the reading; xi is one reading; x̄ is the sample mean; n is the number of readings.
   - s is the sample standard deviation, in the reading's units; sx̄ is the standard uncertainty of the mean, also called the standard error; k = 1, 2, 3 gives roughly 68 %, 95 %, 99 % confidence if the scatter is normal.
   - Draw a histogram of the deviations.
   - ua and ub are standard uncertainties of a and b, in their units; uy is the standard uncertainty of the result y, in the same units as y.
   - uy/|y| is the fractional uncertainty of y; ua/a and ub/b are the fractional uncertainties of the inputs.
   - Limiting (guaranteed) errors are the worst case instead.
   - The display resolution adds a rectangular uncertainty of ± half the last digit.
*/
